ash
Usage: sh [-ilsx] [-c 'SCRIPT'] [FILE [ARGS]]

The shell. On this system /bin/sh is busybox ash, and it is the only shell
installed -- there is no second parser to fall back to or to be surprised by.

	-c SCRIPT	run SCRIPT and exit, instead of reading a file or a tty
	-i		interactive: prompt, job control, history
	-s		read the script from standard input
	-l		login shell
	-e		exit at the first command that fails
	-u		treat an unset variable as an error
	-x		trace: print each command before running it

The grammar you will actually use:

	NAME=VALUE		assign; no spaces around the =
	$NAME  ${NAME}		expand it
	"$NAME"			expand it as ONE word, spaces and all
	${NAME:-DEFAULT}	its value, or DEFAULT if unset or empty
	${#NAME}		how many characters long it is
	$(CMD)			run CMD, become its output
	$((2 + 3))		arithmetic
	CMD1 | CMD2		wire stdout into stdin
	CMD > FILE		redirect stdout to FILE, replacing it
	CMD >> FILE		append instead, keeping what is there
	CMD < FILE		read stdin from FILE
	CMD 2> FILE		errors (fd 2) only
	CMD > FILE 2>&1		both channels, in that order
	CMD1 && CMD2		run CMD2 only if CMD1 succeeded
	CMD1 || CMD2		run CMD2 only if CMD1 failed
	CMD &			run in the background
	$?  $$  $!  $#  $@  $0	status, pid, bg pid, argc, args, own name

What ash deliberately does NOT have, because bash is not installed: arrays,
associative arrays, process substitution <(CMD), here-strings <<<, and
[[ ]]. Use [ ] and a pipe. Every one of those omissions is a parser feature
that has carried a vulnerability at some point.

See also: sh, set, export, read, test
